  "historical_output_text": " Imagine a warm day in springtime, and you're enjoying a gentle breeze. You look up and see a layer of fluffy white clouds in the sky. These are called cumulus clouds, and they're like big cotton balls floating above you. These clouds form when warm, moist air from the ground rises into the cooler air higher up in the sky. As the warm air cools, the moisture in it condenses, turning back into tiny water droplets, which we see as clouds.\n\nAs the day progresses, the sun heats the ground, and more warm air rises. If the conditions are just right, these rising air currents can keep the clouds growing and grouping together. When you see these clouds spreading out across the sky in a pattern, covering the sun at times, and creating light and shadow, they are called cumulus congestus or cum",
